class.description:
Returned by [method HEGoAssetNode.get_geo_output]. Where a fetch config describes the wanted attributes and splits ahead of time in a resource, this describes them in code, at the point of use:
[codeblock]
var output = await _await_task(asset_node.get_geo_output())
await _await_task(output.load_attributes(["N", "up", "pscale", "hego_spawn", "hego_node_path"]))

var groups = output.filter_by("hego_spawn", 1).split_by("hego_node_path")
for node_path in groups:
    var points = groups[node_path].get_points(["N", "up", "pscale"])
[/codeblock]

The geometry itself never leaves C++. A [HEGoGeoSelection] is a list of point indices; filtering and splitting produce new selections without copying attribute values, and only [method HEGoGeoSelection.get_points] assembles data to hand over.

Attribute values are cached per cook and shared with [method HEGoAssetNode.fetch_points] and [method HEGoAssetNode.fetch_surfaces], so reading the same attribute from two outputs only reaches Houdini once. The cache invalidates itself when the node cooks again.

[method load_attributes] is the only call that talks to the session and therefore the only one that returns a [HEGoTask]; everything else reads memory and returns immediately.
